# Git and Delivery Conventions

## Commits

- Use **Conventional Commits** with concise messages in English: `feat(scope): ...`, `fix(scope): ...`, `docs(scope): ...`, `refactor(scope): ...`, `chore(scope): ...`. Scope is optional.
- **Never** add `Co-Authored-By` or any AI attribution to commits or PR descriptions.

## Secrets hygiene

- **Never** stage or commit `.env`, API keys, tokens, or credentials. `.env` is gitignored — keep it that way.
- Check `git status` and `git diff` **before** staging anything; stage only intended files.

## Local artifacts

- Do **not** commit `.atl/` (local skill-registry artifacts) unless the change's purpose is those files themselves. It is gitignored context; leave it out of commits.

## Pull requests

- Draft PR content with the project skill `skills/pr-creator/SKILL.md` (four-section format: Description, Changes Made, Impact, Notes), written in **English**. Do not translate code identifiers.
- Save the PR content to `openspec/PR-{next-number}-{slug}.md` (number = next sequential after existing `openspec/PR-*` files).
- The **user pushes and creates the PR on GitHub themselves**. Do not push, open, or merge PRs without an explicit request. (The pr-creator skill produces content only — it is not an execution action.)

## Production delivery

- Deploy is **manual**: paste modules into Apps Script, set Script Properties, create the daily trigger — per the README deploy section.
- **Do not automate deployment (clasp) or any other push to production without explicit user approval.**

## Documentation language

- New documentation artifacts (README sections, specs, PRs, commit messages) are written in **neutral professional English**.
- Reply to the user in the language they write in — documentation language and conversation language are separate contracts.